---
title: "What Causes Flight Delays?"
subtitle: "A look at departures from New York City in 2013"
---

```{r}
#| label: setup
#| include: false

library(nycflights13)
library(dplyr)
library(ggplot2)
```

## A year of New York departures

In 2013, **336,776 flights** departed from New York City's three major
airports: JFK, LaGuardia (LGA), and Newark (EWR). This page looks at when
departures were scheduled and how late they left.

The word *causes* is a big question: these records can reveal patterns and
associations, but they cannot by themselves prove why a particular flight was
delayed. Weather, congestion, and airline operations are possible factors, but
we would need additional data to test their effects.

## Departure time and delay

Each dot below represents one flight with a recorded departure delay. The
horizontal axis is its **scheduled** departure time; the vertical axis is how
many minutes early or late it actually departed. The view is zoomed to delays
between 30 minutes early and 3 hours late so the dense cluster is easier to
see.

```{r}
#| label: fig-scatter
#| fig-cap: "Each point is a flight; the y-axis is zoomed to show the main cluster."
#| fig-width: 9
#| fig-height: 5.5

flights |>
  filter(!is.na(dep_delay)) |>
  mutate(
    scheduled_hour = sched_dep_time %/% 100 +
      (sched_dep_time %% 100) / 60
  ) |>
  ggplot(aes(x = scheduled_hour, y = dep_delay)) +
  geom_point(alpha = 0.08, size = 0.35, color = "#176B87") +
  scale_x_continuous(
    breaks = seq(0, 24, by = 3),
    limits = c(0, 24),
    labels = \(x) sprintf("%02d:00", x)
  ) +
  coord_cartesian(ylim = c(-30, 180)) +
  labs(
    x = "Scheduled departure time",
    y = "Departure delay (minutes)",
    caption = "Flights with missing departure-delay values are omitted."
  ) +
  theme_minimal(base_size = 12)
```

The points are heavily concentrated near zero delay, with a thinner spread of
long delays. Later scheduled flights often appear higher in the plot, but this
pattern is descriptive—not proof that the time of day itself causes delays.

## Thousands of flights, grouped by delay

This histogram summarizes the same flight records in bins. The bar heights
show how many flights fell into each delay range, and the dashed line marks a
15-minute delay.

```{r}
#| label: fig-histogram
#| fig-cap: "Departure-delay counts for 328,521 flights with recorded values."
#| fig-width: 9
#| fig-height: 4.5

flights |>
  filter(!is.na(dep_delay)) |>
  ggplot(aes(x = dep_delay)) +
  geom_histogram(binwidth = 5, fill = "#176B87", color = "white") +
  geom_vline(
    xintercept = 15,
    linetype = "dashed",
    color = "#C4492D",
    linewidth = 0.8
  ) +
  coord_cartesian(xlim = c(-30, 180)) +
  labs(
    x = "Departure delay (minutes; early departures are negative)",
    y = "Number of flights",
    caption = "The x-axis is zoomed to −30 to 180 minutes; the dashed line is 15 minutes late."
  ) +
  theme_minimal(base_size = 12)
```

Of the **328,521 flights with recorded departure delays**, **200,089** left on
time or early, and **70,774** left more than 15 minutes late. The remaining
flights were delayed by 15 minutes or less. This is a useful description of
departures, but it does not identify which factors produced the delays.

## About the data

The data come from the [`nycflights13` R package](https://cran.r-project.org/package=nycflights13).
Each row represents a scheduled flight. Missing departure delays generally
correspond to flights that did not depart, so the charts describe flights with
an observed departure—not cancellations.

